---
title: Self-hosting
description: Run nullish on your own infrastructure with Docker or Podman.
---

nullish is open source, so you can run it on your own servers. This guide shows you how to proceed with Docker or Podman.

{% aside type="caution" %}
nullish is in alpha. Expect breaking changes between versions, and only the latest version
receives security fixes.
{% /aside %}

## Architecture

| Service    | Image               | Role                                                 |
| ---------- | ------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| `postgres` | `postgres:18`       | The database.                                        |
| `server`   | `usenullish/server` | The API. Also runs background jobs and renders PDFs. |
| `client`   | `usenullish/client` | The web app.                                         |
| `caddy`    | `caddy:2`           | Reverse proxy: HTTPS, and `/api` to the server.      |

Files like generated PDFs are kept temporarily in the server's `/data` volume and deleted periodically. Everything else lives in the database.

Images are published on Docker Hub for `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64`, tagged `latest` and by version, e.g. `0.1.0`, `0.1` or `0`. Pin a version so updates happen when you choose.

## Requirements

- A server with Docker or Podman.
- A domain pointing to it, e.g. `nullish.example.com`.

## Set it up

{% steps %}

1. Create a folder with a `.env` file:

   ```sh title=".env"
   NULLISH_DB_USERNAME=nullish
   NULLISH_DB_PASSWORD=a-long-random-password

   # openssl rand -base64 32
   NULLISH_AUTH_SECRET=
   NULLISH_AUTH_URL=https://nullish.example.com/api
   NULLISH_AUTH_SIGN_UP=invite_only
   ```

   {% aside %}
   A `.env` file is the simplest approach, but it keeps your passwords and secret in plain text.
   In production, prefer encrypted secrets, e.g. with Podman or Docker secrets, or with
   [sops-nix](https://github.com/Mic92/sops-nix) or [agenix](https://github.com/ryantm/agenix) if
   you use Nix.
   {% /aside %}

2. Add a `docker-compose.yml`:

   ```yaml title="docker-compose.yml"
   services:
     postgres:
       image: postgres:18
       restart: unless-stopped
       environment:
         POSTGRES_USER: ${NULLISH_DB_USERNAME}
         POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${NULLISH_DB_PASSWORD}
         POSTGRES_DB: nullish
       volumes:
         - postgres-data:/var/lib/postgresql
       healthcheck:
         test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -U ${NULLISH_DB_USERNAME} -d nullish"]
         interval: 5s
         timeout: 5s
         retries: 10

     server:
       image: usenullish/server:0.1.0
       restart: unless-stopped
       depends_on:
         postgres:
           condition: service_healthy
       environment:
         NULLISH_DB_HOST: postgres
         NULLISH_DB_PORT: 5432
         NULLISH_DB_USERNAME: ${NULLISH_DB_USERNAME}
         NULLISH_DB_PASSWORD: ${NULLISH_DB_PASSWORD}
         NULLISH_AUTH_SECRET: ${NULLISH_AUTH_SECRET}
         NULLISH_AUTH_URL: ${NULLISH_AUTH_URL}
         NULLISH_AUTH_SIGN_UP: ${NULLISH_AUTH_SIGN_UP}
       volumes:
         - nullish-data:/data

     client:
       image: usenullish/client:0.1.0
       restart: unless-stopped
       depends_on:
         - server
       environment:
         NULLISH_API_URL: http://server:3000

     caddy:
       image: caddy:2
       restart: unless-stopped
       ports:
         - "80:80"
         - "443:443"
       volumes:
         - ./Caddyfile:/etc/caddy/Caddyfile
         - caddy-data:/data

   volumes:
     postgres-data:
     nullish-data:
     caddy-data:
   ```

3. Add a `Caddyfile`:

   ```txt title="Caddyfile"
   nullish.example.com {
     handle_path /api/* {
       reverse_proxy server:3000
     }

     handle {
       reverse_proxy client:3000
     }
   }
   ```

   {% aside %}
   The web app calls the API under `/api` on its own domain, that's why a proxy is required.
   {% /aside %}

4. Start it:

   ```sh
   docker compose up -d
   ```

5. Open your domain and sign up. First sign-up wins, whatever the sign-up setting. Create your organization: you're its admin.

{% /steps %}

## Configuration

### Database

| Variable              | Required | Description                       |
| --------------------- | -------- | --------------------------------- |
| `NULLISH_DB_HOST`     | yes      | Database host.                    |
| `NULLISH_DB_PORT`     | yes      | Database port.                    |
| `NULLISH_DB_USERNAME` | yes      | Database user.                    |
| `NULLISH_DB_PASSWORD` | yes      | Database password.                |
| `NULLISH_DB_NAME`     | no       | Database name. Default `nullish`. |

### Authentication

| Variable               | Required | Description                                                                                                             |
| ---------------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `NULLISH_AUTH_SECRET`  | yes      | At least 32 characters. Generate it with `openssl rand -base64 32`.                                                     |
| `NULLISH_AUTH_URL`     | yes      | Public URL of the API.                                                                                                  |
| `NULLISH_AUTH_SIGN_UP` | no       | `true` (anyone), `false` (nobody) or `invite_only` (with an invitation to join an organization). Default `invite_only`. |

## Enable the assistant

The assistant needs an LLM. Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint works, e.g. your own model with Ollama or a provider of your choice. Without one, nullish works fine, but the assistant is disabled.

Add the settings to your `.env`:

```sh title=".env"
NULLISH_LLM_URL=http://ollama:11434/v1
NULLISH_LLM_CHAT_MODEL=qwen3.5:4b
NULLISH_LLM_API_KEY=ollama
```

Pass them to the server in `docker-compose.yml`:

```yaml title="docker-compose.yml"
server:
  environment:
    NULLISH_LLM_URL: ${NULLISH_LLM_URL}
    NULLISH_LLM_CHAT_MODEL: ${NULLISH_LLM_CHAT_MODEL}
    NULLISH_LLM_API_KEY: ${NULLISH_LLM_API_KEY}
```

Then restart with `docker compose up -d`. The assistant is enabled once the first three settings below are set.

| Variable                              | Required          | Description                                                 |
| ------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| `NULLISH_LLM_URL`                     | for the assistant | Base URL of the endpoint, e.g. `http://ollama:11434/v1`.    |
| `NULLISH_LLM_CHAT_MODEL`              | for the assistant | Model name.                                                 |
| `NULLISH_LLM_API_KEY`                 | for the assistant | API key. Must not be empty, even for Ollama.                |
| `NULLISH_LLM_CHAT_TOKEN_LIMIT`        | no                | Tokens each user can use per period. Unlimited if unset.    |
| `NULLISH_LLM_CHAT_TOKEN_LIMIT_PERIOD` | no                | `hourly`, `daily`, `weekly` or `monthly`. Default `weekly`. |

{% aside %}
The model needs a large enough context to fit the assistant's instructions and tools. With Ollama,
32k tokens is a good start.
{% /aside %}

## Operate it

- **Updates**: change the version of both images in `docker-compose.yml`, then `docker compose pull && docker compose up -d`. Migrations run on start.
- **Backups**: back up the database, e.g. with `pg_dump`.
- **Health**: `GET /api/health` returns `{"status":"ok"}`.
- **Logs**: `docker compose logs -f server`.

### Command line

The server image includes a small CLI:

```sh
# List users and their organizations
docker compose exec server ./cli user list

# Permanently delete the organizations marked as deleted
docker compose exec server ./cli organization delete --dry-run
```

Organizations marked as deleted are only permanently deleted when you run `organization delete`.
